    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. So basically it's just a way to make sure neither side gets ripped off while the paperwork is being finished? It sounds like a lot of people are just holding onto each other's money for a while.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exactly right — it's essentially mutual protection so the buyer can verify the property transfers properly while the seller knows the buyer's money is real and committed before handing over the keys. In Ontario, your lawyer typically holds the funds \"in trust\" during closing, and Ontario's real estate regulations require deposits to be held in insured trust accounts, often through the brokerage or lawyer. Once everything checks out on closing day, the money moves to the seller and the keys come to you — it's a well-oiled system, just dressed up in formal paperwork.",
      "source": "target",
